  faqs: [
    { question: "How do I find a good IB tutor in South Korea?", answer: "Matching on the exact school type and subject level works better than a general search, since a foreign-school DP student, a Jeju Global Education City student and a Daegu Korean-language DP student are in genuinely different situations. Tell us the school, current grades and where things are actually going wrong, and IB Gram proposes a specific tutor rather than a directory to search yourself. Reach ibgram24@gmail.com or +91 7439 368 115 on WhatsApp with those details." },
    { question: "Can my child have lessons scheduled to Korea Standard Time?", answer: "Yes. Every session books against KST, which runs year-round with no daylight-saving shift to track, unlike many countries families relocate from. Most Korea-based families choose weekday evenings after school and any hagwon commitments, or weekend mornings, and tutors hold those slots consistently. Tell us the window that works and we match a tutor whose own schedule fits it." },
    { question: "How much does IB tutoring cost in South Korea, and how do we pay?", answer: "Cost tracks the programme level, subject and weekly hours booked — DP Higher Level sciences and mathematics generally cost more per hour than MYP support. A specific rate gets confirmed in writing before any booking, so the first invoice holds no surprise. Send your child's grade, subject and level to ibgram24@gmail.com, and raise currency and payment method in the same message." },
    { question: "Do you have in-person tutors in South Korea?", answer: "No. Every lesson for South Korea runs online; the only place we place a tutor in person is India, where the team is based. Practically that means live video, one-to-one, with a shared digital whiteboard for working problems and past papers, plus written notes to revisit afterward. It matters most for subjects a smaller foreign-school cohort or a Korean-language DP school cannot staff outside class time at all." },
    { question: "My child could stay on Suneung or move into an IB school — how do we decide?", answer: "This is a family and eligibility decision more than a tutoring one, and eligibility often narrows the choice before preference does — most foreign schools restrict Korean-national enrolment to those with qualifying time abroad, while Jeju Global Education City and Daegu's Korean-language DP schools are open more broadly. Students planning an overseas university application generally benefit from IB or Cambridge's international recognition; students committed to a Korean university's mainstream admission usually stay on Suneung. We tutor IB and Cambridge students either way and can speak candidly about workload differences." },
    { question: "Can you help with Cambridge IGCSE if we live in South Korea?", answer: "Yes. A number of Korea's foreign schools, including Seoul Foreign School, teach Cambridge IGCSE alongside or ahead of the IB Diploma. We tutor Cambridge and Edexcel IGCSE subjects against the same syllabus your child's school follows, using that board's own past papers and mark schemes. Send us the syllabus code from the course outline — it tells us the exact variant and exam series being prepared for." },
    { question: "Do your tutors teach both HL and SL subjects?", answer: "Yes. HL and SL are not the same course taught at different speeds — HL adds genuine extra content and sets harder, longer papers, so an HL lesson spends real time on that extension material and on managing the clock under pressure. SL tutoring stays focused on locking in the core syllabus and building steady exam habits. Flag the level for each subject when booking, since strength in one doesn't automatically carry over to the other." },
    { question: "Can a tutor help my child with their Extended Essay or internal assessment?", answer: "Yes, inside limits the IB sets firmly. A tutor can talk through narrowing a research question, walk through what the assessment criteria actually reward, sanity-check whether an argument holds together, and comment on a draft your child wrote on their own. What a tutor won't do is write, rewrite or hand over text for your child to submit as their own — the finished piece has to be theirs, and the school supervisor's guidance always overrides ours." },
    { question: "How do you check your tutors before matching one with my child?", answer: "Vetting covers two things before anyone teaches: genuine subject depth, tested in an interview, and a track record with the specific curriculum they'll be assigned — IB, Cambridge or the Korean-language DP track. We're checking they know this year's syllabus and criteria specifically, not just that they studied the subject once. Should a match not work out once lessons start, tell us and we'll reassign without debate." },
    { question: "Is there a trial lesson before we commit to anything?", answer: "There is. Before any lessons are booked, your child and a proposed tutor spend one session on real material so you can both judge the fit. Watch for the things that actually predict a good match: clear explanations, real familiarity with your specific school's syllabus, and a lesson time that genuinely holds up on your family's schedule. A different tutor is one message away if it doesn't feel right." },
    { question: "Which IB and Cambridge subjects do you actually cover?", answer: "Across DP groups 1 to 5, that means both Mathematics routes, the three sciences, Economics, Business Management, History, Geography, Computer Science, English A and B, Korean A and Environmental Systems and Societies, alongside Theory of Knowledge and matching MYP, PYP and Cambridge IGCSE support. Rarer language or arts combinations depend on who's available at the time, so name the exact subject and level from the timetable and we'll confirm it directly." },
    { question: "Do you offer intensive tutoring before the May IB exams?", answer: "We do. Since the May series is what most Korea-based schools sit, the shift from one lesson a week to two or three usually starts around February and builds from there — full timed papers, mark-scheme walkthroughs, and a tight focus on whatever the mocks flagged as weak. The spring calendar fills quickly, so it's worth locking that increase in early rather than waiting." },
    { question: "Will an IB Diploma help my child get into a Korean university?", answer: "It can, through the special admission tracks Korean universities run for overseas Koreans and foreign nationals, which read IB, A Level or SAT results instead of a Suneung score. These tracks vary by university and faculty and carry their own competition, so confirm the specific requirements for each target university and programme directly, ideally before Grade 10 subject choices are locked in." },
    { question: "Can a Korean national with no time abroad attend an IB school in Korea?", answer: "Two routes exist. Jeju Global Education City's international schools admit Korean nationals without the residency-abroad requirement most foreign schools apply, under a special free-economic-zone policy. Separately, a small number of public schools in Daegu and Jeju now teach the DP directly in Korean as domestic public schools, open through the normal public-school admissions route for their area rather than foreign-school eligibility rules." },
    { question: "How do we get started with a tutor?", answer: "Write to ibgram24@gmail.com or message +91 7439 368 115 on WhatsApp with your child's grade and school type, the subjects and levels in question, hours that work on Korea Standard Time, and the specific outcome you're aiming for. From there we name a tutor, share their background, and set up a first lesson — payment only comes up once you've actually seen that lesson and agreed on a rate." },
  ],

